\begin{remark}
\label{re:gap-W}
If $C$ is a non-hyperelliptic curve of genus  $g \geq 3$ and $C\hookrightarrow  {\mathbb{P}}^{g-1}$ 
is a canonical embedding, then 
$$
a_i=i+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}\alpha_j, \quad 1\leq i\leq g,
$$
are the gap values of $p$. In other words,  $a_{1},\ldots,a_{g}$ are the only $g$ integers where  {\bf  there does not exist}
 a meromorphic function of  $C$ with a pole of order $a_i$ in the point $p$ and holomorphic on $C-\{p\}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{ej:cla-fer}
In the case of a generalized Fermat curve of the type  $(k,2)$, $k\geq 4$, (a classic Fermat curve), 
it is known that (see \cite{Has50,Roh82,Wat99}) the weight of a point $p\in F$ is
$w(p)=\frac{1}{24}(k-1)(k-2)(k-3)(k+4)$, which shows that equality in Theorem \ref{th:w-peso} holds for the classic case.
\end{remark}

Before to go in to the proof of Theorem \ref{th:w-peso}, we discuss two examples. In the first one we observe that the equality, in the previous theorem, holds for a generalized Fermat curve of type $(2,4)$ and in the second one we provide examples on which the bound is not sharp.

\begin{example}
\label{ej:(2,4)}
Let  $C$ be a generalized Fermat curve of the type $(k,n)=(2,4)$, and  $p$ be in $F$. As $H^{0}(C,\omega_C)\cong H^{0}(C,\mathcal{O}_C(1))={\mathbb{C}}[x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4]_1$  (forms of degree $1$ of the polynomial ring  ${\mathbb{C}}[x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3]$),
It follows that the map $\xymatrix{ C \ar[r]^{f_c}&{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}$ is the canonical embedding.
Using the normal form of $f_{c}$ in $p$, we obtain that the gap values of $p$ are
 $a_1=1$, $a_2=2$, $a_3=3$, $a_4=5$, $a_5=7$. In this way,
 $\hat{w}(p)=w(p)=3$. Observe that, by virtue of Theorem \ref{th:hyperosc}, in this case the Weierstrass points are exactly the points of hyperosculation $F$.  
 \end{example}


